as lockdown restrictions begin to ease  care homes across the country are trying  to find a balance between keeping their  residents safe  and reintroducing visits from family  members and  loved ones anna selby is the director of  operations at  sunrise senior living uk and graceful  healthcare's covert 19 task force  she joins us from buckingham and bob  skinner as a resident  at sunrise of cardiff care home welcome  to  you both and achieve first of all um  what has it been  like running care homes during the the  pandemic explain for us  what you've had to go through yeah so it  well it's been a year  of um of lots of changes and lots of  adjustments we've had to  to really think through a lot of  problems and and find ways of  maintaining  safety in our homes and supporting our  residents to still  have um a quality of life so it's it's  been challenging but it's also been um  you know we recognize that we're able to  still give people that quality of life  and still keep them connected and  they're just the way that we work  um and that's kind of where i'm coming  to with this today is that we  we've had to be agile our residents have  been so resilient and  and brave and have um coped with all  kinds of things all kinds of changes  and and i feel that as we get to this  stage of the pandemic  there's another change to get to get  over and we've got to we've got to get  ready  for for coming out of conflict and and  we've got to maintain  as you say that balance of keeping  people safe but giving them  um what they deserve in their life in  what could be  you know the last couple of years of  somebody's life  and has it been difficult to explaining  to some of your residents exactly  you know what what's been going on for  the past year  i think um i mean no even people have  understood that they've what they've had  to i think it's the length of time and  i'm sure bob will talk about this i  think it's the length of time that this  has gone on i don't know if we knew a  year ago  where we'd be today so i think um  i don't think it's come as a surprise at  this point that that um  you know we've had to take the measures  that we've had to because we've  there's a there's a really deadly virus  out there that's particularly dangerous  for this  this age group um but i think that um  yeah our residents have adjusted as  they've coped they've you know they're  very resilient people they've  they've talked about how they felt  they've shared with us so one of the  most important things for us in terms of  our um planning through this has been  listening to people listening to our  residents listening to our family  members particularly  who can explain the things that we don't  necessarily see so that we can address  how people are feeling inside as well as  keeping them safe from the infection  that's raging  outside bob i wonder if you can explain  for us uh what it's been like for you  for the last year  well it's been a miserable year for  everyone um  dark in fact twists and turns  uh ups and downs you've never known from  one week to another  what's going to happen uh we've had our  hopes  raised and dashed as i say but we've  come through it  and the news now is the magnificent i  think  um the best for months to be able to see  our families  to have our families here after all  these months  is great um i spent a month in hospital  luckily i recovered  uh and since i haven't had a visitor for  about six months uh but tomorrow one of  my granddaughters  is coming and that's the end of it now  it's getting better  we're delighted and it's i'm so pleased  for  all the all the people here the staff  the carers at cardiff sunrise have been  absolutely brilliant from day one  it's been a long terrible year but the  it's getting better and we've got good  reason to be cheerful  um i don't know if you remember if you  used to be on holiday we'd send  postcards wish you were here  well that's what we've been doing all  the year wishing  our families we're here but we've been  lucky  because we've got email uh we've got  zoom today i'm speaking to my son later  today  so it's made a big difference but i  realized that  there are so many elderly people  especially the ones with  mental troubles or um alzheimer's  who've had to struggle but they've been  helped through it  and because the carers and the help  we've all had  it's good to see daylight coming again  it won't be very long i'm sure before  we all get back to a normal life which  will be so much better after all we've  been through  no no one's have no one's sad we are  looked after we're happy  and all together i can't say other than  i'm happy to be here  well bob i tell you what you've  certainly cheered uh my spirits what a  positive  outlook i mean that's one of the the  most positive um  summaries of of what we've been through  and what we should be looking ahead to  uh and anna i mean hearing from bob  there  really that there is light at the end of  the tunnel we're almost there aren't we  yes we're almost there i mean we we have  to be careful still you know we  we're not out of the woods yet but that  we should be working our way out of the  woods and we should be as positive  as bob is around what we can do next and  get ready for the next stage get ready  to adjust  and really show um the world that living  in a care home is  is good for people that people can come  out of this and resume their life as it  was  and i think um you know a lot of people  have been  shielded and isolated and lonely at home  uh you know  in our homes people haven't been alone  so and we're in a really fortunate  position that we can support that  change again this time around so that  we're facing the world and that people  have really connecting with like bob  says with people that they haven't seen  for a long time  and uh it's it's a wonderful it's  wonderful we just have to make sure  people aren't overwhelmed that people  can adjust but it's a wonderful  position to be in to know that we're  going to be able to start connecting  people in a meaningful way again very  soon  and bob just finally to you what are you  most looking forward to  uh we're looking ahead we're steaming  ahead  it's going to be happy days ahead in  fact i've got a  first visit for six months my gran one  of my granddaughters ria  is coming tomorrow afternoon i couldn't  be happier  happy days ahead for you there bob thank  you so much for speaking to us bob and  anna  you
 

